  "language": "en",
  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "Send a photo over Wi-Fi. The file travels through air as radio signals. First, your device divides the data into packets. Each packet includes addressing and error checking information. The radio maps groups of bits onto changes in a carrier wave. Wi-Fi also splits the data across many closely spaced subcarriers using a method called OFDM. These radio signals share the available channel with other nearby devices. A device listens before transmitting and adjusts when the channel is busy. The receiver decodes the sub-carriers, checks the packets, and requests re-transmission if needed. Correct packets are reassembled into the original photo. Wi-Fi turns data into carefully organized radio waves, then turns those waves back into data.",
